  "body": "Fixes #13: `short_qa.py` only recorded a frozen span when a later frame\nresumed motion, so a render that stalls and repeats its last frame\nthrough the end of the export was silently dropped from the report.\n\nThe frozen-span loop now flushes a run that is still open when the\ndiff array ends, using the same 12-transition threshold:\n\n```python\nif run_start is not None and len(diff) - run_start >= 12:\n    frozen.append((run_start, len(diff)))\n```\n\nThe span end is `len(diff)`, matching the existing `(start, first-motion-frame)`\nconvention, so the issue's cases come out as reported:\n\n| Input diff | Before | After |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `[1.0] + [0.0] * 12 + [1.0]` | `[(1, 13)]` | `[(1, 13)]` |\n| `[1.0] + [0.0] * 12` | `[]` | `[(1, 13)]` |\n| `[1.0] + [0.0] * 11` | `[]` | `[]` (below threshold) |\n\nAlso adds `templates/scripts/test_short_qa.py`: three stdlib-unittest\ncases that build the same three synthetic videos with ffmpeg, run the\nreal script end to end, and read the frozen spans from `report.md`\n(`python3 templates/scripts/test_short_qa.py`, 3/3 pass).\n\nHow I tested it:\n- Built the three synthetic videos above and ran `short_qa.py` on each\n  before the fix: the trailing-freeze video reported `[]`, the\n  mid-video freeze `[(1, 13)]`, the short tail `[]` — reproducing #13.\n- After the fix, the trailing-freeze video reports `[(1, 13)]` and the\n  other two are unchanged.\n- The new test fails on the old code (`[]` vs `[(1, 13)]`) and passes\n  with the fix.\n",
